[#:seon.task{:name "a-trivial-form-waits-on-two-durable-commits"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A one-form MCP run costs 155 ms warm in the JVM (168 ms through the bridge), 128 ms of it two durable Datahike commits (submission in submit-source!, settlement in settle-batch!), 64 ms each; evaluation is 1-2 ms. Under the ruled durability (sync-blob? and commit-graph? false inadmissible) a commit on this workstation cannot go below ~30 ms of forces, so a single-digit-ms form needs the owner to choose: keep two durable commits and trim (~115 ms), make the submission a non-durable hand-off so one commit per form remains (~60 ms), or relax durability for evaluation records. Options priced in the landing."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/submit-source! seon.cluster.agent/evaluate-source! seon.turn/settle-batch!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-eval-overhead-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-escaped-pass-closes-the-reply-without-its-fault"
             :kind :function
             :intent "When a turn pass escapes after seon.fault/fault! committed, turn-step's finally (agent.clj ~515-558) closes the reply and the caller gets :seon.await/closed-error with no fault, although the receipt is on the throw's ex-data (:seon.fault/recorded). The causing agent meets the fault only in its next, unrelated run. Offer the recorded receipt (or the fault value) on the reply before closing it. Done when a regression whose pass escapes after recording a fault receives that fault as data in the same reply."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/turn-step seon.cluster.agent/evaluate-source! seon.fault/carried-receipt}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-wrapped-stop-interrupt-is-recorded-as-a-core-fault"
             :kind :function
             :intent "turn-step catches only a bare InterruptedException (agent.clj ~551); a stop that interrupts a Datahike call surfaces as superv.async ExceptionInfo(InterruptedException) and is recorded as a core fault with no function entity, which never resolves (477a97dc, 67029fe7, 2026-09-27 02:08/02:25Z). Recognise the stop's interrupt anywhere in the bounded cause chain (cyclic chains: seon.fault/carried-receipt-hangs-on-cyclic-cause-chain). The same faults lost their agent: fault! puts :seon.agent/id into :seon.error/source (fault.clj ~286-292) but not into the recording request, so :seon.error.occurrence/agent is nil (error.clj ~1492) and for-agent's occurrence clause (error.clj ~2066) never reaches the agent; pass agent-id into the recording request. Done when a stop interrupting a wrapped Datahike call records no fault, and a fault recorded under an agent carries that agent on its occurrence."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/turn-step seon.fault/fault!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "interrupted-rewalks-the-cause-chain",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "seon.cluster.agent/interrupted? walks .getCause with its own seen-set loop, a second copy of seon.error.refusal/cause-links. Replace the loop with (some #(instance? InterruptedException %) (refusal/cause-links failure)), EQUIVALENT on every chain (both stop at the first repeat)."}
 #:seon.task{:name "reply-size-is-measured-by-printing-the-sources",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "submit-source-in-projection records :seon.turn/reply-size as (count (pr-str sources)): it prints the segmented sources to count characters, a printer use outside the value printer on every MCP SCI evaluation. Correct: the size is the submitted text's length (the reply it stands for), computed without printing.",
            :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/submit-source!},
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-read-notes.md"}}]
